WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.540
So last week we began the class with the definition of cooking

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.930
from the Oxford English Dictionary, who defined cooking

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:13.590
as the transition of food under the application of heat.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.200
You will recall that we recast that definition as a plot

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.600
because we're scientists, and we like to make plots.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.720
And so we plotted the temperature on an axis.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:22.680
And we drew a line.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.870
And we said below some critical temperature the food is uncooked,

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.740
and above that critical temperature the food is cooked.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.410
We also are able to, on that plot, put different foods.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.840
And so, for example, here is the phase diagram of an egg.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.100
So you know that below some critical temperature an egg is uncooked.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.710
An uncooked egg is essentially a liquid.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.280
You can sort of shake it up and down and feel that it's a liquid inside.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.520
Whereas a cooked egg, when you cook it above some critical temperature,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.005
it becomes a solid.

00:00:49.005 --> 00:00:51.710
So I'm going to just flash up a couple of different materials

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.460
and show you what the behavior looks like.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.370
And then we can try to put this all into a broader perspective.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.810
This is the phase diagram for water.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.860
There is a critical temperature, which is about 0 degrees Celsius,

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.099
below which water is a solid.

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:06.030
It's ice.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:11.010
And there's a critical temperature above which water transforms from a liquid

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.650
to a gas, and you all know that that occurs

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.040
at about 100 degrees Celsius, at least if the pressure is

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.050
one atmosphere, about sea level.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:22.590
So nitrogen has a phase behavior that looks actually, for all intents

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.170
and purposes, a lot like that of water.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.840
At room temperature, nitrogen is actually a gas.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.694
You can make nitrogen into a liquid if you cool it enough.

00:01:30.694 --> 00:01:32.610
And it turns out that the temperature at which

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:37.650
nitrogen transitions from a gas to a liquid is about minus 195 degrees

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:38.550
Celsius.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.270
So it's very, very cold.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:44.080
If you cool it a little more to about minus 210 degrees Celsius,

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.120
then nitrogen will become a solid.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.970
So here's another material that's important for cooking.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:49.990
This is ethanol.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.170
So ethanol, of course, is something that many people like to drink.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:56.790
If you're enjoying or not enjoying today's lecture with a glass of wine

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:02.020
in your hand, which is recommended by the way, then the wine, of course,

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.320
has some amount of ethanol that's in it.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.600
So the temperature at which ethanol transitions

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.080
from being a liquid to a gas occurs at a slightly lower temperature

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.110
than that of water.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.860
It happens at about 78 degrees Celsius.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.510
That's why alcohol boils off more quickly from a dish than water,

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.690
if you're ever using wine or something to make sauces.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.035
Ethanol also can become a solid.

00:02:23.035 --> 00:02:25.910
And to do that, you have to cool it more than you have to cool water.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:30.510
The temperature at which ethanol becomes a solid is about minus 114 degrees

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:31.370
Celsius.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.120
But the point that I want you to take away

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.995
is that the general phase behavior of ethanol

00:02:34.995 --> 00:02:38.857
also is exactly like that of water, and it's exactly like that of nitrogen.

00:02:38.857 --> 00:02:41.190
It's just that the transition temperatures are changing.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:45.230
And in particular, it looks nothing like that of an egg.

